 # TASK-CHAIN
 
-A chain is a few ordinary task entities, created together, linked by `:seon.task/next`, worked one after another by
-separate agents on one branch, and merged to main once at the end. Root finds every chain through one fact on main and
-reads its branch directly. Everything is data in the database; root's listener reacts to it. No copies, no subject
-task, no per-step merges, no automatic relaunch. A single task is a chain of one: there is one launch path.
+**Target (task-model T1–T4, owner 2026-10-17).** Tasks are desired state on main: a task names what it `needs`
+(`:seon.task/needs`, a set), its environment (`:seon.task/branch`, `:seon.task/target`, `:seon.task/config`,
+`:seon.task/model`) and whether it should run (`:seon.task/execution`). Observed state is facts written by their
+observer when the event happens: a graph process, an attempt and its end, a judged done, a merge, a test result, a branch
+head. One reconciler, `seon.cluster.agent/reconcile`, a pure function of (desired, observed), answers the minimal actions;
+each action's actor writes the observed fact that closes its gap, and that commit wakes the next pass. There is no chain
+entity and no chain fact: discovery, order, branch, config and merge each live on a task. Every written task is `:held`
+and nothing autoruns; root's `my.root/run!` is the one release, and `:seon.config.agent/max-running` caps the open
+attempts. A merge is an ordinary task after the work it merges (§2.6). A single task is a task with no needs: there is
+one launch path. "Chain" survives only as plain prose for tasks written together.
+
+**Installed until task-model T3b/T3c.** A chain is a few ordinary task entities, created together, linked by
+`:seon.task/next`, worked one after another by separate agents on one branch, and merged to main once at the end. Root
+finds every chain through one fact on main (`:seon.chain/*`) and reads its branch directly. Everything is data in the
+database; root's listener reacts to it. No copies, no subject task, no per-step merges, no automatic relaunch. A single
+task is a chain of one. The rest of this card states the installed model and, beside it, the target.
 
